s, to every single blood cell of their body, overriding the importance of a nervous system and of its respective organs and nerves into these cells, and allowing them to behave as a kind of çhivemindé, where all of them are organized, aligned and çth\inkingé in the same way as the original body would. When inside a contaminated body, no visible alterations can be seen, as the contamination is assymptomatic in nature, however, when expelled from the body through deep cuts, dismemberment, draining or an\other method, the blood cells can be voluntarily separated from the blood plasma as per the command of the affectedês conscience, acquiring a malleable, smoke and flour-like consistency, able to either freely move, regenerate the wound and come back to th\e original body or fully leave and enter another susceptible body, alive or not. Healthy cells from another individual, if contaminated with cells with the virus, result in the individual having his conscience overwritten with the original hostês, but if \said hostês cells are destroyed, the hostês conscience is also destroyed, with the virus, however, remaining in its basic state. The virus itself can only be destroyed through extreme temperatures \(4532oF or more\). The village, when faced with the entit\y, assimilated its properties into their culture, building a cult following around it, and basing their lifestyle around the god that was responsible for the gift of the çWinds of Lifeé. With an idol they created using a transmission supercatalyst, compos\ed of a decorated glass pot with a mix of the contaminated air with stem cells of people of the first generation of the village, kept in constant circular movement to avoid stagnation, the inhabitants lead the normal lifestyle of a common village, disposi\ng of houses, temples, stores and dojos. The main use they have for the virus is the systematic transferring of their conscience into new generation bodies, considered by them as a çresurrectioné, however, successive resurrections ended up gradually alter\ing the structure of their bodies, with the current inhabitants having severe deformities akin to abominations. Objects can also çstoreé the conscience of the resurrected, provided they have an organic composition, therefore, some of them, when facing dea\th, decide to resuscitate, for example, as their own houses if theyêre made of wood, or table idols, or other types of animals or plants, according either to the will of the affected, or the villageês necessities.)/CreationDate(D:-001-1-1-1-1-1-00'30')/DA(0 0 0 rg /Arial 6 Tf)/DS(font: Arial 6.0pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/M(D:20240920215250-03'00')/NM(d7acb930cdb148c288da8a71ac95f1c3)/Page 1/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.3.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"  style="font-size:6.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal\;font-family:Arial;font-stretch:normal"><p dir="ltr"><span style="text-align:justify">The resident entity of the Seinokaze village, founded 1600 years ago and localized in the territory belonging to the Doikijinese embassy in Dropflight, in the Fifth Glob\e War timeline, consists of an airborne transmissible virus, that can be spread through pure air, but usually utilizes stray organic cells as catalysts. Currently having contaminated all of the village&#8217;s inhabitants, keeping it permanently quarantin\ed by the adjacent villages and cities, and consequently isolating it from the world, the virus&#8217; anomalous property is inherently linked to its existence, leading to all of the contaminated beings to also be treated as anomalies and being extremely \recommended to non-infected being extreme distance from all possible means of transmission, and any opportunities for so to be immediately and discretely terminated. Its anomalous property consists of an unknown modification of its composition, of ontolog\ical style, which allows for the conscience of the affected to be &#8220;transferred&#8221;, through unknown methods, to every single blood cell of their body, overriding the importance of a nervous system and of its respective organs and nerves into thes\e cells, and allowing them to behave as a kind of &#8220;hivemind&#8221;, where all of them are organized, aligned and &#8220;thinking&#8221; in the same way as the original body would. When inside a contaminated body, no visible alterations can be seen, \as the contamination is assymptomatic in nature, however, when expelled from the body through deep cuts, dismemberment, draining or another method, the blood cells can be voluntarily separated from the blood plasma as per the command of the affected&#8217\;s conscience, acquiring a malleable, smoke and flour-like consistency, able to either freely move, regenerate the wound and come back to the original body or fully leave and enter another susceptible body, alive or not. Healthy cells from another individ\ual, if contaminated with cells with the virus, result in the individual having his conscience overwritten with the original host&#8217;s, but if said host&#8217;s cells are destroyed, the host&#8217;s conscience is also destroyed, with the virus, however\, remaining in its basic state. The virus itself can only be destroyed through extreme temperatures \(4532</span><span style="font-size:9.1pt;text-align:justify;vertical-align:+4.5pt">o</span><span style="text-align:justify">F or more\). The village, when\ faced with the entity, assimilated its properties into their culture, building a cult following around it, and basing their lifestyle around the god that was responsible for the gift of the &#8220;Winds of Life&#8221;. With an idol they created using a t\ransmission supercatalyst, composed of a decorated glass pot with a mix of the contaminated air with stem cells of people of the first generation of the village, kept in constant circular movement to avoid stagnation, the inhabitants lead the normal lifes\tyle of a common village, disposing of houses, temples, stores and dojos. The main use they have for the virus is the systematic transferring of their conscience into new generation bodies, considered by them as a &#8220;resurrection&#8221;, however, succ\essive resurrections ended up gradually altering the structure of their bodies, with the current inhabitants having severe deformities akin to abominations. Objects can also &#8220;store&#8221; the conscience of the resurrected, provided they have an orga\nic composition, therefore, some of them, when facing death, decide to resuscitate, for example, as their own houses if they&#8217;re made of wood, or table idols, or other types of animals or plants, according either to the will of the affected, or the v\illage&#8217;s necessities.</span></p></body>)/Rect[45.3301 67.854 571.302 206.874]/Subj(Text Box)/Subtype/FreeText/T( )/Type/Annot>>endobj116 0 obj<</BS<</W 0>>/CA 1/Contents(   Dif)/DA(0 0 0 RG 0 0 0 rg 0 Tc 0 Tw 100 Tz 0 TL 0 Ts 0 Tr /Helv 21.8 Tf)/DS(font: Helvetica,sans-serif 21.8pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/M(D:20240916144237-03'00')/NM(1136aaeffc294a48afa27816b1ee9af3)/Page 2/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.3.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"  style="font-size:21.8pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:norma\l;font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-stretch:normal"><p dir="ltr"><span style="font-family:Helvetica">   Dif</span></p></body>)/Rect[17.301 674.202 180.873 699.612]/Subj(Text Box)/Subtype/FreeText/T( )/Type/Annot>>endobj117 0 obj<</BS<</W 0>>/CA 1/Contents(   Dif)/DA(0 0 0 RG 0 0 0 rg 0 Tc 0 Tw 100 Tz 0 TL 0 Ts 0 Tr /Helv 21.8 Tf)/DS(font: Helvetica,sans-serif 21.8pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/M(D:20240916144242-03'00')/NM(1219d7225def45e683de28447d5ac6ff)/Page 2/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?